WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.277
Git is easier once you have seen what it stores. The Git lab is one server with a repository already made: real branches, a merge history, a resolved conflict, a remote. We read it from underneath, then practise on it.

00:00:16.377 --> 00:00:31.756
One cloud image; the install builds the repository for your user and a bare remote beside it.

00:00:31.856 --> 00:01:12.346
A repository is a .git directory: objects, refs, HEAD, config. The bare remote has exactly the same files and no working tree: that is all a Git server is.

00:01:12.446 --> 00:01:27.481
The history as a graph. Then cat-file opens a commit: a tree, two parents, an author, a message. Two parents is what makes it a merge: nothing else marks it.

00:01:27.581 --> 00:01:41.712
The tree is a directory listing: mode, type, hash, name. The blob is the file's bytes and nothing else: no name, no history. The name lives in the tree.

00:01:41.812 --> 00:01:54.190
A branch is a file with one hash in it. HEAD is a file naming the branch you are on. Creating a branch copies nothing; that is why it is instant.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:02:07.211
Merges: when both sides moved, Git writes a commit with two parents. feature/hello was merged like that; show prints the two parents.

00:02:07.311 --> 00:02:26.877
Now a conflict, made on purpose: two branches change the same line. The merge stops and Git writes both versions between markers.

00:02:26.977 --> 00:02:41.595
You decide the content, add it, commit: the merge commit records the resolution. Then back to where we were.

00:02:41.695 --> 00:03:04.758
Stash shelves a change you are not ready to commit: the tree is clean, the change waits on a stack, pop brings it back.

00:03:04.858 --> 00:03:28.232
The remote. fetch updates your copy of its branches; these two ranges answer the only questions that matter: what is there and not here, what is here and not there.

00:03:28.332 --> 00:03:42.654
Rebase replays commits on top of another branch: feature/stash sits on main's hotfix in a straight line, no merge commit. New hashes: never rebase what others already have.

00:03:42.754 --> 00:04:00.566
Git flow gives branches roles: develop integrates, features finish into it, a release finishes into main and leaves a tag. The lab's release 1.0 is tagged v1.0.

00:04:00.666 --> 00:04:08.652
The exercises change history on purpose. One script rebuilds the whole repository in seconds.

00:04:08.752 --> 00:04:23.370
Nine tests, one per exercise, check the repository is as the lab hands it to you.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:31.300 line:6%
Every command is in the guide, in English and Italian, from the lab's card.
